- `action(n2, "do you desire new hair"), action(n4, "do you want your hair washed"), action(n6, "do you want your hair cut"), action(n8, "do you want your hair dried")`: Incorrectly typed as `action` nodes; the source describes these as decision gates ("if you desire/want..."), not executable process steps, so their payload meaning should drive branch/guard logic instead of being modeled as tasks. (SMC2)
- `branch(b1)` through `branch(b4)` lack corresponding `guard(...)` statements: The source explicitly defines conditional transitions ("if you desire new hair", "if you want your hair washed", etc.), but the model omits guards to evaluate these conditions, rendering the XOR splits non-deterministic and omitting documented decision behavior. (SMC1, SMC4)
- `defines(v1, "do you desire new hair", {}), defines(v2, ..., {}), defines(v3, ..., {}), defines(v4, ..., {})`: All variable ranges are empty `{}`; the source's conditional logic requires valid value spaces (e.g., `{true, false}` or `{"Yes", "No"}`) to faithfully represent the "if" decisions. (SMC6)
- `precedence(n3, c1)` combined with `precedence(b1, n4)`: Violates source chronology and logical dependency; executing `n3` ("visit the hairdresser") jumps directly to `converge(c1)`, bypassing the subsequent wash/cut/dry evaluations, while branching from `b1` to `n4` permits service checks without first visiting the hairdresser, contradicting the sequential "visit... If you want... have it..." flow. (SMC3, SMC4)

